2026-11-16 Yu-Sung Su     <suyusung@tsinghua.edu.cn>
      * R/binnedplot, man/binnedplot: the plus and minus 2 SE band is the
        model-based 2*sqrt(p*(1-p)/n) of Gelman and Hill (2007, section 5.6) by
        default; se = "empirical" keeps the old band, which collapsed in
        low-probability bins because it used the sd of the residuals in the bin.
        The help example moves to the response scale (issue #53).
      * R/bayesglm: the Warning argument is read at last.  It was accepted,
        passed down and documented as controlling the non-convergence and
        separation messages, and was ignored.
      * R/bayesglm: the printed unnormalized log posterior passed the prior mean
        to dt()'s non-centrality argument, ignored the prior scale and used
        invlogit() whatever the link, and its log likelihood ignored the binomial
        totals.  It now uses the scaled-t prior and the family's link, and matches
        logLik().
      * R/bayesglm, R/model.matrixBayes: predict(type = "terms", newdata = ) no
        longer fails with "invalid 'labels'".  model.matrixBayes() copied
        attr(object, "intercept") onto its terms and a fitted model has none, so
        it copied NULL and the design matrix came back without an intercept.
      * R/bayesglm: a rank-deficient fit with a prior too flat to carry weight no
        longer fails with "object of type 'closure' is not subsettable".  The prior
        is doing nothing in that case, so the fit is handed to glm.fit(), which
        returns NA for the aliased coefficient and numbers for the rest.
      * R/bayesglm: a start of the wrong length reports the length it wanted
        instead of "the condition has length > 1".
      * R/bayesglm: with scaled = TRUE the prior scales, the augmentation means
        and the dispersion update are computed with the prior weights, so a weight
        of 2 now means the same as a duplicated row.
      * R/bayespolr: start is read as the documented c(coefficients, zeta) instead
        of in the optimiser's log-gap parameterisation, and a zeta that does not
        increase is rejected.
      * R/bayespolr: maxit bounds the optim() run as well as the start-value fit,
        and the cloglog start values come from a cloglog fit rather than a probit
        one.
      * R/sim: sim() dispatches on plm and coxph objects.  sim.plm() no longer
        multiplied the already-scaled vcov(<plm>) by sigma^2, and sim(lm) keeps
        aliased coefficients as 0 the way sim(glm) does.
      * R/balance: the raw sd-ratio column is computed rather than left NA, a
        matrix without column names no longer errors, and ... reaches the plot
        when plot = TRUE.
      * R/display: the unreachable .Call("mer_sigma") branch is gone, and an
        intercept-only lmer keeps its "(Intercept)" coefficient name.
      * R/rescale: a constant is centred instead of returning NaN.
      * R/dwish: coerces W, not S.
      * R/go, man/go.Rd: document the .R extension the function appends.
      * R/coefplot: any CI draws coefficients plus and minus CI sds, not just
        plus and minus 1 sd; the help page says so.
      * R/discrete.histogram: handles a single distinct value, a factor with an
        unused level, and ylab = passed through the dots.
      * R/triangleplot: the legend digit search no longer stops with "Too many
        digits!" on close-together correlations.
      * R/traceplot: a bugs trace plot labels its vertical axis "Kept draws", the
        index the axis actually carries, rather than "iteration".
      * man/multicomp.plot.Rd: documents abbreviate(label, minlength = 2) rather
        than "the first 2 characters".
      * tests/: 23 new regression-test files, one per finding above; each fails on
        the previous master with its own message.
      * R/bayesglm, R/model.matrixBayes: a formula whose term is a transformation
        of a variable -- poly(psi, ...), log(x) -- no longer fails with "object
        'psi' not found" when the fit also has weights.  model.matrixBayes() used
        to subset the model frame it was handed, which drops the frame's terms
        attribute and the columns the terms refer to (a frame holds the evaluated
        poly() column, not psi).  Found by the reverse-dependency check of 1.15-4:
        qgcomp's bayes path failed on it.
      * R/model.matrixBayes, R/bayesglm: model.matrixBayes(formula, data) still
        returns no intercept column -- a formula carries no intercept attribute to
        copy -- because FindIt prepends its own intercept and broke when that
        changed.  predict(type = "terms", newdata = ) is fixed in its caller
        instead, which now builds the contributions from the fit's terms and model
        frame.
      * tests/: two more regression-test files for those, and a test for the
        shape model.matrixBayes(formula, data) returns.

2026-11-15 Gaurav Sood     (correctness audit; index in issue #54)
      * R/bayespolr: scaled = TRUE now rescales the prior as documented
        (prior.scale/range(x) for a two-valued predictor, prior.scale/(2*sd(x))
        otherwise).  The block was guarded by xint > 1, where xint is the column
        of the intercept, so it never ran (pull request #29).
      * R/bayespolr: deviance (and so logLik and AIC) is that of the data.  It was
        twice the optimised objective, which also holds the log prior and the
        prior pseudo-counts.  Fitted probabilities now include the offset, as in
        MASS::polr (pull request #30).
      * R/bayesglm: for a gaussian response the x-scaling of the prior is kept on
        every iteration.  It was undone after the first, so estimates depended on
        the units of x (pull request #46).
      * R/bayesglm: AIC and logLik for a binomial response with more than one
        trial pass the trials vector to family$aic, as glm.fit does (pull request #40).
      * R/bayesglm: the design matrix is built from the model frame, so subset,
        na.action and drop.unused.levels are honoured (pull request #41).
      * R/bayesglm: the stored terms are built with keep.order, so predict(se.fit =
        TRUE) and type = "terms" pair columns with the right coefficients (pull
        request #44).
      * R/bayesglm: the dispersion update for families other than binomial and
        poisson uses only rows with positive weight; with scaled = FALSE a zero
        weight equals dropping the row (pull request #42).
      * R/bayesglm: an offset combined with a vector prior no longer errors in the
        null-deviance refit (pull request #43).
      * R/bayesglm: control = list(maxit = ) is honoured when maxit is not given
        (pull request #45).
      * R/extractDIC, R/display: DIC for merMod fits is the conditional DIC computed
        from sim() draws, pD = mean deviance - deviance at the mean draw.  The old
        pD was 0 for ML lmer fits and negative for glmer fits.  extractDIC() gains
        n.sims and returns pD as an attribute; display() uses a fixed internal seed
        and restores the caller's (pull request #52).
      * R/display: for glmer fits without a scale parameter, no Residual sd row
        (pull request #51); for svyglm, n is the number of observations, not the
        design degrees of freedom (pull request #50).
      * R/balance: the matched weights are applied to treated units as well as
        controls, which matters for IPTW, ATE and ATC (pull request #35).
        plot.balance() subsets longcovnames with which.covs (pull request #36);
        print(combined = TRUE) reads diff.means.matched (pull request #37).
      * R/matching: replace = FALSE stops when there are fewer controls than
        treated units, instead of returning NA matches (pull request #38).
      * R/standardize: the data and variables are found in the environment where
        the model was fitted, not in arm's namespace or the global environment
        (pull request #39).
      * R/standardize: stops with an explanation when rescaling makes a transformed
        input undefined (e.g. log() of a centred variable), instead of silently
        dropping rows; standardize() no longer errors when nothing is rescaled
        (pull request #55).
      * R/sim: n.sims = 1 for glm no longer returns zeros; an intercept-only polr
        no longer puts a cutpoint into the coef slot (pull request #48).
      * R/sigma.hat: sigma.hat() for a glm returns sqrt(dispersion) for every
        family; it returned NULL for all but gaussian (pull request #47).
      * R/se.coef: se.coef() and se.ranef() handle a list-valued postVar, as for
        two random-effect terms on one grouping factor (pull request #49).
      * R/coefplot: the axis range covers user-supplied confidence bounds, which
        also no longer require sds (pull request #31); the bugs method honours
        pch.pts (pull request #32).
      * R/binnedplot: binned.resids() returns the bin breakpoints in xbreaks
        (pull request #33).
      * man: coefplot, binnedplot, matching, contrasts.bayes, mcsamp and bayesglm
        corrected where they disagreed with the code (pull request #34).
      * DESCRIPTION: URL lists the GitHub repository (pull request #28).
      * tests: a regression test for each code change above (all except #28 and
        #34), each checked to fail on the code it guards.

2026-11-07 Yu-Sung Su     <suyusung@tsinghua.edu.cn>
      * DESCRIPTION: (Version, Date): 1.15-4
      * R/rescale: "binary.inputs = -0.5,0.5" now maps the two observed values onto -0.5
        and 0.5 whatever the variable is coded as.  It previously subtracted 0.5 from x,
        which is only correct for a variable already coded 0/1, so a numeric binary
        variable coded 1/2 gave 0.5 and 1.5.  That is the same bug as issue #19, which
        had been fixed for factors only.
      * R/rescale: an unrecognised "binary.inputs" is now an error.  For a binary
        variable a value outside the four documented options fell through every
        branch and returned NULL invisibly, and for a continuous variable it was
        silently ignored.
      * man/rescale: state the binary.inputs options precisely, including that "0/1" and
        "-0.5,0.5" are invariant to how the variable is coded.
      * tests: first regression suite, run by R CMD check.  Plain R scripts on purpose,
        so there is no new dependency.  tests/simmer.R pins the sim() covariance fix
        (issue #26), tests/load-first.R the .onAttach guard (issue #25),
        tests/standardize.R the formula-in-an-object fix (issue #24) and tests/rescale.R
        the binary handling above.  Each was checked to FAIL against the code it guards,
        not merely to pass against the fix.
      * README.md: rewritten.  It was a title and a single line; it now describes
        what the package is for, how to install it, the parts most people want
        (bayesglm, display, standardize/rescale, sim, the plots, matching) with
        their output, two example figures drawn from the shipped lalonde data,
        and a grouped function index.

2026-11-07 Yu-Sung Su     <suyusung@tsinghua.edu.cn>
      * DESCRIPTION: license changed from GPL (>= 2) to GPL (>= 3), i.e. GPL-3.0-or-later.
      * R/simmer: fix sim() for merMod, whose random-effect draws did not match the model's
        covariance: the fill-reducing permutation of the sparse Cholesky factor was never
        undone, and Lambda was transposed (issue #26; fix contributed by David Skalinder
        in pull request #27).
